== Random networks: Ford–Fulkerson (any path), fattest path, Edmonds–Karp vs brute-force minimum cut
OK   FF (any path), fattest path and Edmonds–Karp all return a feasible flow of value = brute-force minimum cut (2500 random reduced networks, 2–7 vertices)
OK   every flow is integral (integrality theorem); each augmentation adds an integer F >= 1, f' feasible, |f'| = |f| + F
OK   at the end G_f has no s-t path; S = vertices reachable from s is a cut of capacity |f|, its S->T edges saturated and its T->S edges avoided
OK   that S is contained in the source side of every minimum cut (the smallest minimum cut)
OK   Lemma 10.1 on every intermediate flow and every cut: |f| <= ||S,T||, equality iff saturate S->T and avoid T->S
OK   net flow across every cut (out of S minus into S) equals |f|; ∂f(s) = |f| = -∂f(t)
OK   Ford–Fulkerson with integer capacities makes at most |f*| augmentations
OK   fattest rule: best-first search finds the maximum-bottleneck path (= brute force over simple paths)
OK   fattest rule: F >= (|f*| - |f|)/E at every step, and at most max(1, ceil(E ln|f*|)) augmentations
OK   the maximum flow of G_f has value |f*| - |f| at every step (brute-force minimum cut of G_f)
OK   Lemma 10.2: BFS levels in the residual graph never decrease (Edmonds–Karp)
OK   Lemma 10.3: an edge disappears only as a bottleneck of the augmenting path, at least one per iteration, and each residual edge at most V/2 times (max seen 1)
OK   Edmonds–Karp makes at most EV augmentations (largest ratio to the book's EV/2 seen: 1.000)
== Subdividing edges preserves the maximum flow and the minimum cut
OK   400 random networks with antiparallel edges: subdividing every edge gives the same maximum-flow value and minimum-cut capacity
== Flow decomposition
OK   decomposition algorithm: the pieces add up to f, are simple s-t paths and simple cycles, and number at most #f (edges with positive flow) — random flows built from paths and cycles
OK   an edge lies on some piece iff it carries positive flow; every weight is positive
OK   circulations decompose into cycles only, at most max{0, #f - 1}; acyclic flows into paths only
OK   alpha f + beta g is an (s,t)-flow of value alpha|f| + beta|g| (random rational alpha, beta, flows of any sign)
OK   the book's walk-from-s-until-t version gets stuck on the circulation s a t b s (path s a t first, then t->b->s has nowhere to go) and on a flow of value 1; the page's version decomposes both
OK   keeping only the paths of a maximum flow's decomposition gives a maximum flow (300 networks)
OK   a non-negative flow of value -1 (one unit on t->s) is a flow; paths have value 1 and cycles 0, so no positive combination of them gives it
OK   path flow with 1 on the path's edges is a flow of value 1; the book's extra -1 on reversed edges breaks conservation when u->v and v->u are both edges
OK   the book's path flow (with -1 on reversed edges) of an augmenting path in a reduced network is a flow of value 1, and f + F·P is the augmented flow  [every augmenting path of every intermediate flow, 400 networks]  [54 backward edges used]
OK   the book's walk-from-s-until-t decomposition never gets stuck when no flow enters s: Ford–Fulkerson flows (any, shortest, fattest paths) carry no flow into s, and on them and on random such flows it returns at most #f positive pieces adding up to f  [600 flows]
OK   page: the book's version is credited where it works (no flow into s, Ford–Fulkerson's flows) and the -1 clause where it fits (augmenting paths)
OK   cancelling positive cycles one at a time keeps the value and feasibility, empties an edge each time and ends with an acyclic maximum flow (400 networks)
OK   with a non-shortest augmenting path, BFS levels can decrease: s->v, s->a, a->b, b->u, u->v (1), v->t (2); augmenting s a b u v t lowers level(u) from 3 to 2 (Lemma 10.2 needs shortest paths)
OK   Edmonds–Karp on 600 networks with 6–10 vertices: correct value, levels monotone, each residual edge disappears <= V/2 times (max seen 1), <= EV iterations (largest ratio to EV/2: 0.133)
