== Zwick's network (Figure 10.8) in exact arithmetic over Q(sqrt 5)
OK   phi = (sqrt5 - 1)/2 satisfies 1 - phi = phi^2
OK   Zwick's network is reduced, with six X edges and horizontal capacities 1, 1, phi
OK   its maximum flow (minimum cut) is 2X + 1 (X = 10)
OK   every path of the sequence central, (B, C, B, A) x 8 is an augmenting path at its turn
OK   the X edges are never the bottleneck: their residual capacity stays >= X - 3, flow on them <= 3
OK   table: from phi^(k-1), 0, phi^k the steps B, C, B, A push phi^k, phi^k, phi^(k+1), phi^(k+1) and leave (phi^(k+1), phi^k, 0), (phi^(k+1), 0, phi^k), (0, phi^(k+1), phi^(k+2)), (phi^(k+1), 0, phi^(k+2)); k = 1, 3, 5, ... (8 rounds)
OK   after 4n + 1 augmentations the horizontal residual capacities are phi^(2n), 0, phi^(2n+1) (n = 0..8); the book's phi^(2n-2), 0, phi^(2n-1) never matches
OK   flow value after 4n + 1 augmentations = 1 + 2(phi + ... + phi^(2n)) = (2 + sqrt5) - 2 phi^(2n+1)/(1 - phi)
OK   the limit 1 + 2 sum_{i>=1} phi^i equals 2 + sqrt5 (about 4.236), not the book's 1 + 2/(1 - phi) = 4 + sqrt5
OK   the flow values increase strictly and stay below 2 + sqrt5, while the maximum flow is 2X + 1 = 21
